  WHAT THIS REPORT EVIDENCES
    This test compiles a real decision-table spreadsheet and executes it
    through a stateless rule-engine session, so it is the archive's direct tie
    to the rule-engine expression-language floor advance from 2.4.7.Final to
    2.4.15.Final - the highest-impact runtime fix in the backend track. The
    installed version throws a bytecode verification error from its own
    bytecode-based accessor optimizer, reproduced even against a bean compiled
    for the older release, so the defect is in the library's generated code
    rather than in what it reads. The floor was found by bisection: 2.4.8
    fails, 2.4.9 works, and no changelog identifies the fixing release. The
    rule-engine compiler declares that expression language with no version of
    its own, so the reactor version property governs what actually loads. Both
    ends of that advance were re-verified in this repository: the base commit
    declares 2.4.7.Final and the current tree declares 2.4.15.Final, through a
    single managed entry whose version resolves from the reactor property.
    Recording a floor rather than a latest-available release is itself an R-1
    obligation, since the requirement is the minimum capable version.
    Consuming surface, re-derived: zero .drl rule files in the tree, and 39
    drools-*.xlsx decision tables (command: find . -name 'drools-*.xlsx'
    -not -path '*/node_modules/*' | wc -l). The migration plan quotes 43; the
    extra four are three .xls files plus one excel_4.xlsx test fixture, all
    four of which were located and confirmed to be unrelated test fixtures, so
    39 plus 3 plus 1 reconciles the plan's figure exactly.
    The spreadsheet this test loads is /rules/drools-access-control-rules.xlsx,
    read from the CLASSPATH. Verified: the sole copy lives in
    acm-services/acm-service-data-access-control/src/main/resources/rules/, and
    that module is NOT a direct dependency of this plugin - the resource
    arrives transitively. The test asserts the resource exists before it
    compiles it, so it probes transitive classpath composition as well as the
    rule-engine execution path, and the migration perturbs exactly that
    composition through a new managed exclusion and a split persistence-provider
    bytecode-library version axis. This module also owns
    drools-complaint-number-rules.xlsx, the table behind the generated-number
    smoke flow, and it declares the decision-table artifact directly even
    though the spreadsheet reaches it transitively.
    One further property of this class is worth recording: it is the only unit
    test in this module that uses no mocking library at all, which matters
    because the mocking-library advance from 4.1 to 5.6.0 touches the other
    seven. Re-verified by counting occurrences across all eight unit tests.

  NOT BYTE-COMPARABLE
